## What happened

Apple's security frameworks (`Security.framework` / `CryptoKit`) **dropped DSA support entirely**. .NET on macOS delegates to the platform's native crypto libraries, so once that support was removed upstream, `DSA.Create()` throws **`PlatformNotSupportedException`** on macOS.

In .NET 10 this still worked because the runtime carried a fallback path, but .NET 11 removed that fallback, surfacing the platform limitation directly.

## How to fix it

Since you only need to **verify** existing legacy signatures (not generate new ones), you have a few options:

| Option | Tradeoff |
|---|---|
| **Use BouncyCastle** (`Portable.BouncyCastle`) | Pure managed code, works everywhere, no native dependency |
| **Bundle OpenSSL on macOS** and P/Invoke into it | More complex packaging |
| **Move verification server-side** (Linux/Windows) | Architecture change |

### Recommended: BouncyCastle

```csharp
// dotnet add package Portable.BouncyCastle

using Org.BouncyCastle.Crypto;
using Org.BouncyCastle.Crypto.Parameters;
using Org.BouncyCastle.Security;

public static bool VerifyDsaSignature(byte[] publicKeyBytes, byte[] message, byte[] signature)
{
    var publicKey = PublicKeyFactory.CreateKey(publicKeyBytes);
    var verifier = SignerUtilities.GetSigner("SHA256withDSA");
    verifier.Init(forSigning: false, publicKey);
    verifier.BlockUpdate(message, 0, message.Length);
    return verifier.VerifySignature(signature);
}
```

This is a **managed implementation** — no platform crypto dependency, so it works identically on Linux, Windows, and macOS. It's the lowest-risk path for a verification-only use case.
